e45ff16f781f4903d5ad6a1d12a64" "q-42258/selection/b9acb975bd321126de384ba941f486f7f5ce7720a51fddf5203cc988d930a350"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-42258" :question/id #uuid "4cbd1e16-e623-56fd-8563-e7c6cd4b5ff2" :question/math-academy-id "q-42258" :question/problem "Calculate $ABC$, where\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 3 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, recall the associative law\n\n$$\nABC=(AB)C=A(BC).\n$$\n\nNotice that $A$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix, $B$ is a $2\\times 3$ matrix, and $C$ is a $3\\times 1$ matrix. Therefore, \n\n- multiplying $A$ by $B$, we will obtain a $3\\times 3$ matrix, which will require finding $9$ entries, while\n\n- multiplying $B$ by $C$, we will obtain a $2\\times 1$ matrix, which will require finding $2$ entries.\n\nSo, we find the product $BC$ first: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}BC & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ -10\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply $A$ by $BC:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ABC & =A(BC) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 3 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ -10\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-68 \\\\ -22 \\\\ 50\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-42258/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8feeacff-b07d-5f98-a765-4d8571674a7a"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-42258"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-42265"] :question/problem "Given that $A,B,$ and $C$ are $4\\times 4$ matrices, which of the following statements are always true?\n\n- $AB=BA$\n- $(AB)C=A(BC)$\n- $AC+BC=(A+B)C$" :question/worked-solution "Let's consider the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is false. Matrix multiplication is not commutative, so $AB\\ne BA$ in general.\n\n- Statement II is true. Matrix multiplication is associative, so $(AB)C=A(BC)$ in general.\n\n- Statement III is true. Matrix multiplication is distributive, so $(A+B)C=AC+BC,$ and $A(B+C)=AB+AC$ in general.\n\nTherefore, only statements II and III are true." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-130313/selection/b8dc6bd704c0aeb06f6a77d09092f32130e25c8d8b0bc0bbfbe5c1d3b60cc79c" :answer/id #uuid "fab7f6ae-164e-50cf-95f9-81545d92e939" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 & -3 & -9 \\\\ -8 & 8 & 24 \\\\ 7 & -7 & -21\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-130313/selection/af9b8b6b76586a8f323069d24d6ca66f2ad0f009d87a7b96d15912080325e950" :answer/id #uuid "5c12bf6f-0686-5a12-a788-8b2b087181b8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 & 6 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-130313/selection/57367c4a3b33db6546e5f2a6c678abba223df4f2e0d2eb2127ae16043e697cd3" :answer/id #uuid "eb5e3af3-359c-573c-8659-8adb9a64f010" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-8 & 8 & 24 \\\\ 3 & -3 & -9 \\\\ 7 & -7 & -21\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-130313/selection/40a8d434831048e5125bf7121e04a43d35d7e79ea6a75565eb9e0d2bf2c2cb5d" :answer/id #uuid "daf48bcd-3e22-538c-a048-7768bf351c0a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 & -8 & 7 \\\\ -3 & 8 & -7 \\\\ -9 & 24 & -21\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-130313/selection/a56e719ab3be54cd4ee3581710bb965d88a94cac2d3ecf71490509f9c4b03cd2" :answer/id #uuid "86895714-6b91-571c-afb5-3c9a38e025dc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}7 & -7 & -21 \\\\ 3 & -3 & -9 \\\\ -8 & 8 & 24\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db